 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>RecipeTin’s recipe uses <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"250\" data-s=\"0\">250 g</span> thinly sliced Chinese barbecue pork (char siu), added ready-cooked at the end.</li><li>Her notes suggest diced chicken (or bacon, ham or pork) as a substitute; cooking it separately for 3–4 minutes after the shrimp is this page’s step, not hers.</li><li>With char siu, skip step 4 and add the sliced pork with the shrimp.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Chicken instead of char siu"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>Raw shrimp hang straight; perfectly cooked ones curl into a “C”; overcooked ones curl tight into an “O”.</p>",
   "title_html": "Is the shrimp cooked?"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>Any generic curry powder; hot if you like the spice.</p>",
   "title_html": "Curry powder"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>Swap for dry sherry, cooking sake or mirin — or chicken broth if you don’t cook with alcohol.</p>",
   "title_html": "Shaoxing wine"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>RecipeTin lists “<span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"cup\" data-a=\"1\" data-s=\"0\">1 cup</span> red capsicum” without a cut; thin strips (matching the onion) are assumed here.</p>",
   "title_html": "Capsicum cut"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>For hot buckwheat noodles, see <a href=\"curry-soba.html\">curry soba</a> — the curry flavour travels well.</p>",
   "title_html": "Soba instead?"
  }
 ],
